The test reveals issues:
1. "in band 2s" set depth = ceiling+3 after above-ceiling excursion, ceiling was ~? At the above ceiling state, n2 increased? Wait, n2 went from 40 to 41, ceiling ~108.7. Then set depth = ceiling+3 = 111.7, but the readout says 109.5 and on:true. Because the simulation moves the diver (buoyancy/input?) and the ceiling changes. The band is C-5 to C+0.6; depth=C+3 is *below* the band (deeper), so on-gassing. We should have set depth = C-2. My mistake.

2. Bends stayed at 35 after 2s in the band (not recovering) because `run.aboveCeiling` was false then, so recovery should happen at 1.15/s → should drop to 32.7. But it shows 35.2 (same). Wait, `run.aboveCeiling` is set false at the start of the N2 block, then if the branch is absorb, it remains false; then `if (!run.aboveCeiling) bends -= 1.15dt`. Over 2s, bends should drop 2.3. It didn't. Why? Possibly `run.bends` was 35.2 and the readout after 2s is 35.2 — maybe the update didn't run because the browser tab was throttled? But n2 changed from 41 to 41.0 similarly. The simulation might not be running at full 60fps in headless? It is. Or the page lost focus and paused? No, state dive. Hmm, maybe the "in band" snapshot happened after the depth was set but the bend value persisted because the `if (!run.aboveCeiling)` is after the branch; should run.

Wait, maybe `run.bends` is not the same because I didn't set `r.bends`? It was 35 from the above-ceiling test. The next read after 2s still 35.2. That suggests recovery is zero. Let me check the code:

```
if (depthAboveCeiling > 0.6) { ... }
else if (run.depth >= ceiling - 5 && run.depth > 0.9) { ... offgas }
else { aboveCeiling ... }
if (!run.aboveCeiling) {
